## Reference assemblies on msil-ld's command line (ImplementationPlan.md
## 5.11.1, 5.9.3, 5.9.4, 5.11.2; M21.a items 2-5; texts of 5.9.14 and 5.11.9):
## - --reference=<path> and --reference <path> (EEq, repeatable) take a file
##   or a directory; a directory contributes every '*.dll' in it that has CLI
##   metadata, not recursively, and skips other files silently (here a text
##   file and a native DLL, both named '*.dll', and a '.txt' file); an
##   assembly named Second.exe and a core library in a subdirectory, both a
##   second core library, would make the link fail if they were read;
## - an explicitly named file without CLI metadata is
##   '<path>: not a .NET assembly', also when it lies in a directory that is
##   given too, in either order (a directory skips it silently, an explicit
##   name does not);
## - the same path twice, or a file and its directory, is read once: no
##   diagnostic, the same output;
## - a positional .NET assembly without the toolchain's symbol table is a
##   reference too, so positional and --reference inputs give equal PEs
##   (M21 retires M20's error for such inputs);
## - a library built by this toolchain (an Assembly-row uint8[] attribute of
##   class '<msil>SymbolTable', Inputs/fakepack/ToolLib.il) is skipped under
##   --reference with the warning
##   '<path> is a library built by this toolchain; pass it as a link input instead'
##   and given positionally is, from M22, a library input (5.9.15): the
##   program gets its AssemblyRef (version 0.0.0.0, no key) and the
##   'ldtoken [ToolLib]ToolLib' that initializes it, though it uses nothing
##   of it (shared-*.test have the rest);
## - a .NET assembly inside an archive, a reference or a toolchain library,
##   is not a link input and never a reference (section 12 row 2026-10-26,
##   '5.9.4 and 5.9.14 amended (.NET assemblies in archives ...)'): each such
##   member gives the warning
##   '<archive>(<member>): .NET assemblies in archives are not linked; ignored'
##   with or without --whole-archive, and the link goes on without it, so it
##   uses the built-in table and equals the link without the archive (refs.a
##   holds the pack's three DLLs, tool.a toollib.dll, mix.a the CIL object
##   helper.o, which main-helper.o needs, and FakeCore.dll);
## - -r stays M14's error;
## - --print-bcl-table needs no input and prints the built-in [mscorlib]
##   table (5.11.7): one line per mscorlib-safe BCLMembers.def entry in .def
##   order, 'type ' or 'method ' and cli::printBCLMemberRef's text for scope
##   mscorlib. Those are M18's entries (System.ValueType, the four owner
##   types, the five Phase 1 methods), then M20's (StringToHGlobalUni,
##   EntryPointNotFoundException and its constructor, System.Object); M21's
##   NativeLibrary entries are not mscorlib-safe and are not listed; then
##   M22's six attribute entries (M22.a item 6: System.Attribute, its
##   constructor, System.AttributeUsageAttribute, System.AttributeTargets,
##   AttributeUsageAttribute's constructor and set_AllowMultiple), whose
##   parameter lists print as BCLMembers.def writes them.
## The pack is Inputs/fakepack (gen.py): FakeCore (the core library),
## FakeInterop (Marshal) and FakeThreading (Interlocked). p1.o requests every
## Phase 1 member (data with a relocation, a variable-length memmove, a fence
## and main(int, char **)), so the link binds all three; with the pack its
## listing equals the listing without references up to scopes.

